From: rehanraza786 <rehanraza786@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 01:56:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/12] Anchor assertions to the repo that owns their evidence
 file
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

assert_edge's description promises "git-committed and reviewable, exactly
like an ADR". In a multi-root workspace that was not true: both writers put
docs/graph-assertions.json at the workspace parent, which is not a git repo,
so the file was loaded by the indexer but never versioned, never reviewed,
and never shared — the control silently absent exactly where a team has the
most repos. save_decision already guards against this for ADRs.

Paths are repo-prefixed, so the first segment of the evidence file names the
repo. assert_edge and annotate now anchor there (assertionsBase /
_assertions_base), and both editions report the real path they wrote.

Readers take the parent AND every root: the indexer, and the
ariadne://assertions resource, which otherwise under-reports what is
actually in the graph. Existing files at the parent keep working, so this
needs no migration.

Lifecycle follows. retract and reaffirm search every candidate file and edit
whichever one holds the record, instead of assuming the parent. A dismissal
is keyed on a gap, not a file, so there is no repo to infer: it honors an
explicit root and otherwise stays at the workspace root, saying out loud
that it will not be versioned there.

One consequence worth knowing: assert_edge now legitimately dirties the
worktree of the repo it writes into, until you commit — which is what the
tool has always told you to do. The old placement hid that by writing
somewhere git could not see, which is the bug.

run_tests.py: the annotate lifecycle now asserts against the anchored file,
and both protocol probes commit what assert_edge wrote, the way a user
would.
